WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.972
You write a SQL query.

00:00:04.972 --> 00:00:07.944
It joins three tables.

00:00:07.944 --> 00:00:10.916
Milliseconds later you have your answer.

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:13.889
But in between, a tiny program did something profound.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:16.861
It looked at every way to run your query, estimated the cost of each, and picked the cheapest.

00:00:16.861 --> 00:00:19.833
That program is the query optimizer.

00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:22.805
Watch what it does.

00:00:22.805 --> 00:00:26.104
Five stages, left to right.

00:00:26.104 --> 00:00:29.403
Stage one.

00:00:29.403 --> 00:00:32.703
SQL is declarative.

00:00:32.703 --> 00:00:36.002
It states the result, not the procedure.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:39.301
It says what, not how.

00:00:39.301 --> 00:00:42.600
Stage two, parsing and binding.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.899
The text becomes a tree, table and column names resolve against the catalog, and the query is now valid.

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:49.198
Stage three, the logical plan.

00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:52.498
This is relational algebra, and every rewrite preserves the answer.

00:00:52.498 --> 00:00:55.797
Push filters down before joins.

00:00:55.797 --> 00:00:59.096
Drop unused columns early.

00:00:59.096 --> 00:01:02.395
Flatten subqueries.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.694
The result is now pinned.

00:01:05.694 --> 00:01:08.993
Every plan after this returns the same tuples, so only running time can still change.

00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:12.293
Stage four, the physical plan, where speed is decided.

00:01:12.293 --> 00:01:15.592
It picks a join order, a join algorithm, and an access path.

00:01:15.592 --> 00:01:18.891
Join orders explode.

00:01:18.891 --> 00:01:22.190
Three tables give six orders, ten tables give three point six million.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.489
So it uses dynamic programming.

00:01:25.489 --> 00:01:28.788
Compute the cheapest sub-plan for each subset of tables once, then reuse it.

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:32.088
N factorial work collapses to about two to the N.

00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:35.387
Each candidate is costed from table statistics.

00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:38.686
Stage five, execute.

00:01:38.686 --> 00:01:41.985
Only the winner executes, routinely a hundred times faster than the naive ordering.

00:01:41.985 --> 00:01:45.822
The optimizer does not think in SQL.

00:01:45.822 --> 00:01:49.658
It thinks in lego blocks.

00:01:49.658 --> 00:01:53.495
Four families.

00:01:53.495 --> 00:01:57.331
Access methods, how to fetch rows.

00:01:57.331 --> 00:02:01.168
Sequential scan for more than ten percent of rows, index scan for under one percent, bitmap scan in between.

00:02:01.168 --> 00:02:05.005
Partition and sort, how to break big into small.

00:02:05.005 --> 00:02:08.841
Hash partition, or BigSort.

00:02:08.841 --> 00:02:12.678
Aggregation, how to collapse to one row per group.

00:02:12.678 --> 00:02:16.515
Hash aggregate, or sort aggregate.

00:02:16.515 --> 00:02:20.351
Join algorithms, how to combine two tables.

00:02:20.351 --> 00:02:24.188
Hash join, sort-merge join, or block nested loop.

00:02:24.188 --> 00:02:28.024
Any physical plan is just one choice from each family wired together.

00:02:28.024 --> 00:02:31.861
Each block is a primitive you already studied on its own page, so plan selection is really a cost comparison across these combinations.

00:02:31.861 --> 00:02:38.045
The optimizer is one of the most sophisticated pieces of software you will use without ever seeing it.

00:02:38.045 --> 00:02:44.229
You write declarative SQL, it figures out the rest.

00:02:44.229 --> 00:02:50.413
When it gets the plan wrong, the cause is almost always bad statistics.

00:02:50.413 --> 00:02:56.598
It thinks Listens has a thousand rows when it really has a billion, picks a plan tuned for small data, and the query takes hours.

00:02:56.598 --> 00:03:02.782
The fix in production is to run analyze, which refreshes the statistics.

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:08.966
Most slow queries in the world come down to stale stats, not bad SQL, and statistics are exactly what we cover next.
